    "description": "The Expression Compiler evaluates a fixed grammar for numeric literals, variable references, addition, multiplication, and negation. The parser currently returns an open node hierarchy, and the evaluator discovers node kinds through repeated runtime type checks and casts. Each new grammar case requires edits in the evaluator, formatter, optimizer, and diagnostics mapper; a forgotten branch causes a runtime failure or an incorrect default result. The compiler team requires one total analysis operation for every node kind, with payload fields bound directly during analysis so the optimizer can distinguish literal values from variable identifiers without accessing unrelated properties. Because the grammar is owned by this compiler and additions are reviewed together, the complete node vocabulary can be enforced in the type system. Decide how the node representation and evaluation flow should ensure that every supported expression is handled and that adding a grammar case forces all relevant analyses to be revisited.",
    "decision_statement": "Choose a node representation and evaluation structure that binds case-specific fields and makes omissions of a supported expression kind a compile-time error.",
    "notes": "pattern-matching primary: total case analysis with payload binding and compile-time coverage over a closed owned grammar; algebraic-data-type and sealed-case-class are the representation near-misses; make-illegal-states-unrepresentable supports."
  },
